UNIT display_value
LANG ts
PRIMARY-SHA256 591b9b343cbc8fb26f23c7863113229a2bc06db31202e34d28b70950cfdd2746
SIG export default function display_value(tv: Json): string | null
ABI TypeScript compiles to the js runtime and is driven exactly as js. Declare in the artifact
  type Json = null | boolean | number | string | readonly Json[] | { readonly [key: string]: Json }
  (the abstract json). text = string; bool = boolean; none = null; list = an array; map = a plain object; "absent" (TABLE
  NODE, R5) = the key is not an own property (Object.hasOwn), "none" = the property holds null. The result is a string, or
  null for R5's top-level ABSENT; carry ABSENT-MISSING and ABSENT-NULL internally as a typed discriminant, never as
  undefined-through-any. A throw (R2, R6, R7) is a thrown Error; the message is not graded. STRICT: the artifact
  type-checks under strict: true with no any, no non-null assertions (!), no @ts-ignore and no @ts-expect-error. ES module
  default export; imports nothing.
ENVELOPE widening as js: a lone surrogate in text is quoted as \u and 4 lowercase hex digits.
MECHANISM (restated from js — language files are self-contained) QUOTE (R4) is exactly JSON.stringify on a string. Fields
  are read with Object.hasOwn after narrowing the node with typeof x === 'object', x !== null and !Array.isArray(x); t must
  be typeof 'string' (R1). FIELDNAME (R11) tests the name against
  PLAIN-IDENTIFIER (pinned below) and otherwise quotes it with JSON.stringify; a bigint (TABLE NODE) is String(v) + "n"
  for a text, bool or null v and the literal "undefinedn" for an absent one. A text entry (R6) is split by code point ([...s]), never by UTF-16 unit.
PINNED regex PLAIN-IDENTIFIER = /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$/ CITED R11 — no flags: no u, no i, no m (with m a name ending in a line feed would pass)
DEPENDS-ON none
PROVENANCE rederive@bb8ded84fa50 cli/sir/canon.mjs display; language file cut 2026-10-27 with the primary.
